// @no-instantiate type error in return
// ACCEPTS, THEN MISCOMPILES. A NARROWED literal union in a PARAMETER position, returned
// through a `string` annotation, is lowered as the i32 ATOM the parameter is repped as
// while the return slot is declared as a string reference. `vl check --codegen` exits 0
// and the module does not validate. The directive matches V8's wording, which is the
// harness engine — `type error in return[0] (expected (ref 1), got i32)`. Wasmtime rejects
// the same module as `type mismatch: expected (ref $type), found i32`; the RETURN SLOT is
// what both name, which is why the pinned text says `return` rather than a valtype.
//
// The narrowing is load-bearing and this is what makes the defect narrow: the two
// neighbouring spellings BOTH lower correctly today.
//
//   type K = "ka" | "kb" | "kc"
//   function takesK(v: K): string { v }              // OK — registered alias
//   function takesInline(v: "ia" | "ib"): string { v } // OK — bare inline union
//   function takesN(v: K & !"kb"): string { v }      // MISCOMPILES — this file
//
// So the parameter's rep and the widening conversion at the return are agreeing for a
// registered alias and for a bare inline union, and disagreeing only where the union was
// REBUILT by subtraction at a fresh arena index. That is the same fresh-index property
// that `nodeTyIsLitUnionStructural` was introduced to see through on the FIELD ladder
// (tests/cases/structs/inline-litunion-field-atom-rep.vl); this is the PARAMETER-to-return
// half of it, which that change deliberately did not touch.
//
// Found while sizing the population reachable by the field-ladder widening — it reproduces
// identically on the build BEFORE that change, so it is pre-existing and independent.
type K = "ka" | "kb" | "kc"
type N = K & !"kb"
function takesN(v: N): string { v }
function main() { print(takesN("ka")) }
main()
